Here's a couple of nice tips on surviving:
(spoilers)

Engraving 'Elbereth' (especially with an athame) is nice, but you can improve
on that --- you can also keep monsters (any non-'@' sign) away by engraving
'Elbereth' in doorways or along corridors.  It's easy to seal away a room
like that and engravings that you're not actually _on_ seem to last much
longer, if not forever.  You can get the same effect by levitating above an
engraving but levitation is not always available.

So if pursued by an orc horde, engrave 'Elbereth' and keep moving -- that
way you can keep them away long enough to heal, and have the option of
changing armor & other activities which normally seem to tend to blur any
engraving that you're on.

Locking yourself in a room (having a key) is also useful, but has the
disadvantage that you can't fight monsters while the room is locked.
You always end up healing, then opening the door and being crushed by
hordes of monsters, one of which will invariably manage to leave a 
corpse in the doorway which keeps it from being closed and locked again.
In a room guarded by 'Elbereth' you can still stand inside and throw
daggers etc. down the corridors as needed, and move to one side to heal
at times.

BTW it would be an EXCELLENT tactical addition to be able to set traps or
to have engravings that would have other effects besides 'scare monster' ...
perhaps runic engravings that would (when stepped on) confuse monsters, or 
cause conflict.  Put 'em in fortune cookies and change the actual word 
used every game -- as it is, 'Elbereth' is a little too powerful because you 
always know it.

A suggestion for developers while I'm here:

Have occasional out-of-depth monsters generated, such that the probability
of a given monster being 'x' levels out of depth being something like
1/x-squared, and make maybe 1 out of 10 monsters be an out-of-depth 
monster.  You could even have "Worms of the Abyss" whose level of normal
appearance is 61 or some level you never get to, but they could still
pop up once in lower levels.  This would make the game less 'flat',
and give people generating (or waiting for) hordes of monsters at an
altar something to think about.

Also:

Can we PLEASE have some nastier low-level-monsters ... ??  The monster
toughness seems to go up more or less linearly, but the player toughness
goes by a power law -- armor class x weapon power x strength x HP.

Also:

What about a spell of 'degeneration' (disease), castable by players, wands, 
and some monsters?   This would cause the victim to lose 1 or more HP per
round (depending on how many times/how strong the spell was cast).  You'd
get better game play, because you could have a choice of tactics (cast
'degeneration' and run away? or stand and fight?) and you'd have a wide
choice of options to deal with degeneration being cast on you, before
your HP ran out.  That would give you a real use for potions of healing;
it would be another use for prayer; and you could have old carcasses give
you 'degeneration' according to age/worminess of carcass instead of the
current slightly silly & incongruous all-or-nothing 'deathly ill' 
scenario which doesn't really seem like part of the game.



